WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.461 --> 00:00:04.383
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:10.730
RUSS WHITE: Welcome
to Cloud OnAir,

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:12.650
Google Cloud's live webinars.

00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:15.220
We host webinars every Tuesday.

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:16.850
And I'm Russ.

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:19.700
I'm here to talk to you
about Grab and Go, Google's

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:22.760
Chromebook loaner program.

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:24.470
Standby after the session.

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:27.800
You can ask questions, and
we have Googlers on standby

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:30.920
to answer them for you.

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:34.440
So first up, Google's
Grab and Go program.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:37.690
It's a self-service
Chromebook loaner program.

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:40.040
Before we get into the
specifics of the program,

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:42.690
I'm going to tell you a
little bit about Chromebooks

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:44.750
and Chrome OS at Google.

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:48.490
So we're actually Chrome
enterprise's first and largest

00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:49.000
customer.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:52.180
We have the largest fleet
of Chromebooks in the world.

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:55.160
We manage a mixed-hardware
ecosystem here at Google.

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:58.150
So Chrome OS is just one
component of the broader

00:00:58.150 --> 00:00:59.830
suite of applications we have.

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:01.990
We have Linux, Mac,
Windows, you name it--

00:01:01.990 --> 00:01:06.520
whatever our users need to
be happy and productive.

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:08.840
And we're also just
another big enterprise.

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:10.060
We also want to reduce costs.

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:11.290
We want to keep our
users productive.

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:13.331
And we want to make sure
they have the tools they

00:01:13.331 --> 00:01:14.500
need to get their jobs done.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:18.060
The benefits that Chrome
OS brings to our ecosystem

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:20.620
break down into two
high-level categories.

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:22.567
So there's the user
focused benefits.

00:01:22.567 --> 00:01:24.400
We found that our users
are very happy being

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:27.280
able to switch between
devices, work anywhere,

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:29.530
and just generally
use a platform that's

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:31.930
made for working on the cloud.

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:35.440
On the business side, we found
that Chrome's baked-in security

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:38.770
features make it
perfect for making

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:41.260
IT administrators a little
bit happier with the security

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:41.920
state.

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:46.580
And also reduce the burden of
management on these devices.

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:48.070
So it's a great investment.

00:01:48.070 --> 00:01:51.400
They're super easy to
manage, and users love them.

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:54.580
Now it's important to keep in
mind that Grab and Go is just

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:59.890
a small piece of the broader
Chrome OS ecosystem at Google.

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:02.162
Before we get into the
specifics of the program,

00:02:02.162 --> 00:02:03.620
I do want to talk
about the problem

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:05.700
that we were trying to address--

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:07.130
IT downtime.

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:10.660
So IT downtime is actually
a very, very big problem

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:12.440
for enterprises.

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:16.420
IHS Market estimates that North
American companies are spending

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:20.020
around $700 billion a
year on broken devices,

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:22.280
IT downtime, things like that.

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:24.100
Now that might be
maybe a million dollars

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:27.670
for a small- to medium-sized
business or up to $60 million

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:30.250
for a larger enterprise.

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:33.019
That productivity
loss time extends

00:02:33.019 --> 00:02:34.810
beyond just the bottom
line of the company.

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:37.660
There's also
employee motivation,

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:39.520
corporate reputation,
and the pressure

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:40.870
that it puts on IT teams.

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:43.032
So really, IT downtime
is a huge problem.

00:02:43.032 --> 00:02:44.990
That's why we started
digging into this problem

00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:45.781
in the first place.

00:02:47.839 --> 00:02:50.130
Google's certainly not immune
from those same problems.

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:52.884
Again, we're just
another big enterprise.

00:02:52.884 --> 00:02:54.300
The problems that
we saw generally

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:57.130
broke down into these
four categories.

00:02:57.130 --> 00:02:58.980
So the first was the
fatal device error-- so

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:02.760
some software problem that
rendered your device unusable.

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:04.710
Maybe you forgot
your laptop at home.

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:06.240
Maybe you lost it.

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:09.170
Maybe it's just not accessible
for whatever reason.

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:11.131
You might be traveling
to a different location

00:03:11.131 --> 00:03:13.130
where you either don't
want to bring your device

00:03:13.130 --> 00:03:14.660
or can't bring your device.

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:16.790
And then the all-famous
damaged device--

00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:20.150
maybe you've spilled coffee
into the keyboard of your laptop

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:22.170
as you rushed into the
office in the morning.

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:25.091
We've definitely all been there.

00:03:25.091 --> 00:03:26.840
So our first crack at
solving this problem

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:28.790
was with a traditional
loaner program,

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:31.160
or what we call a
traditional loaner program.

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:34.520
So the employee
experiences the problem,

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:38.060
they come to the help desk
seeking a loaner device,

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:40.995
they wait in line, and they
see someone from the help desk.

00:03:40.995 --> 00:03:43.370
The person from the help desk
prepares a device for them,

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:45.270
checks it out,
explains how to use it.

00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:47.900
The employee takes
the device, uses it--

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:49.460
probably with a
lower productivity

00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:51.101
than their usual device.

00:03:51.101 --> 00:03:52.850
And then they return
it when they're done.

00:03:52.850 --> 00:03:54.320
Again, they have
to wait in line.

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:55.910
They have to talk
to the IT admin.

00:03:55.910 --> 00:03:58.220
They have to go through
that whole process.

00:03:58.220 --> 00:04:01.430
The admin takes the device
back, wipes it, resets it,

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:04.400
does whatever they need to do
to prepare it for the next user.

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:06.450
And the cycle repeats.

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:09.560
Now this was definitely
helping solve the problem.

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:11.300
And we definitely
realized some gains

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:13.080
from having a loaner
program like this.

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:15.410
But it still wasn't
cutting it for us.

00:04:15.410 --> 00:04:18.560
Somewhere around 10% of
visits to our help desk

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:20.810
were just for checking
out a loaner device, which

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:23.660
is a huge amount of time that
both Googlers and our IT team

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:25.055
were spending on this.

00:04:25.055 --> 00:04:27.180
Every step in the process,
people were losing time.

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:29.304
And it really wasn't a
great experience for anyone.

00:04:29.304 --> 00:04:32.450
So we took another crack at it.

00:04:32.450 --> 00:04:35.210
That's when we designed
the Grab and Go program.

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:37.100
We designed it with
four core tenets

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:39.919
in mind, first being it
needed to be self-service.

00:04:39.919 --> 00:04:42.210
We didn't want to have all
these IT admin interactions.

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:45.622
We wanted to allow Googlers to
do this whenever they needed.

00:04:45.622 --> 00:04:47.330
Sign-in process needed
to be super quick.

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:48.950
No waiting in line,
no setup, no nothing.

00:04:48.950 --> 00:04:50.720
You just sign it and
you're back to work.

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:52.880
Return process needed
to be just as easy.

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:55.940
And of course, it
needed to be 24/7.

00:04:55.940 --> 00:04:59.656
Let's dig into those core
tenets a little bit deeper.

00:04:59.656 --> 00:05:01.280
First up, self-service--
the whole idea

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:04.180
was that you can help yourself.

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:06.250
The physical manifestation
of our loaner program

00:05:06.250 --> 00:05:09.190
is a shelf, devices,
and signage so that you

00:05:09.190 --> 00:05:11.920
understand how it works.

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:14.650
These shelves where devices
were constantly charging

00:05:14.650 --> 00:05:16.810
are placed throughout
our offices.

00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:19.624
Typically, they're placed in
areas of high-traffic, or maybe

00:05:19.624 --> 00:05:21.790
outside of a help desk,
where people kind of already

00:05:21.790 --> 00:05:24.670
associate IT problems going.

00:05:24.670 --> 00:05:27.130
On those shelves, devices
are always charging.

00:05:27.130 --> 00:05:29.247
So they're ready to go as
soon as you pick it up.

00:05:29.247 --> 00:05:31.330
There's a sign right above
the shelf that explains

00:05:31.330 --> 00:05:32.480
how to use the program.

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:36.241
So basic things like just pick
it up, log in, and get going.

00:05:36.241 --> 00:05:37.990
We found that users
had a lot of questions

00:05:37.990 --> 00:05:39.490
about how the program worked.

00:05:39.490 --> 00:05:42.280
And they were kind of unsure
of the easy nature of it.

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:45.195
So having that sign there really
makes them feel a lot better.

00:05:45.195 --> 00:05:46.570
And then of course
on the devices

00:05:46.570 --> 00:05:48.700
themselves, we also
brand them so that people

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:51.010
know this is a loaner device.

00:05:51.010 --> 00:05:52.480
And we answered some FAQs.

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:54.910
We put stickers inside the
laptop, as well-- for things

00:05:54.910 --> 00:05:56.614
like getting set
up or how to return

00:05:56.614 --> 00:05:58.030
the device-- both
common questions

00:05:58.030 --> 00:06:00.430
that we found with our users.

00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:02.950
One kind of invisible,
magic piece of this

00:06:02.950 --> 00:06:06.430
is, as soon as a user
logs in to the device,

00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:09.130
we push a custom Chrome
companion application

00:06:09.130 --> 00:06:10.300
to the device.

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:13.067
And that reports back to us
that that user has signed in,

00:06:13.067 --> 00:06:14.150
and it assigns it to them.

00:06:14.150 --> 00:06:16.360
And we'll explain that in
a little bit more detail

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:19.379
later in this presentation.

00:06:19.379 --> 00:06:20.670
The next piece-- quick sign-in.

00:06:20.670 --> 00:06:23.550
So like I said, users need to
be able to sign in and get back

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:24.300
to work quickly.

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:28.020
And IT admins didn't really
need to be involved in that.

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:31.050
So Chrome OS actually provided
a lot of the key ingredients

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:32.340
here.

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:36.360
The things like Chrome Sync
and Google's prolific use

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:39.090
of G Suite applications made
Chrome OS an easy choice,

00:06:39.090 --> 00:06:40.335
in this respect.

00:06:40.335 --> 00:06:42.250
A user could just
take it, log in

00:06:42.250 --> 00:06:44.250
with their corporate
account, and they were back

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:45.220
to work just like that.

00:06:48.490 --> 00:06:50.440
Return process needed
to be just as easy.

00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:54.880
Sign out, close the lid, put it
back on the shelf, plug it in,

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:55.990
and you're done.

00:06:55.990 --> 00:06:58.660
Chrome OS again provided
a key ingredient here.

00:06:58.660 --> 00:07:01.180
So we needed to
create a system that

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:03.040
allowed devices to
go from user to user

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:05.380
to user, without IT
admins being involved.

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:07.630
We didn't want to wipe these
devices every single time

00:07:07.630 --> 00:07:08.890
someone used them.

00:07:08.890 --> 00:07:11.530
So Chrome OS has this
feature built into it

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:13.720
where profiles are
encrypted by default.

00:07:13.720 --> 00:07:16.450
So you don't have to worry about
data going from user profile

00:07:16.450 --> 00:07:19.220
to user profile.

00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:21.260
And lastly, 24/7 access.

00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:23.300
Mishaps certainly don't
stick to a schedule.

00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:23.895
Neither do we.

00:07:23.895 --> 00:07:26.270
This loaner service needed to
be out there, barrier-free,

00:07:26.270 --> 00:07:27.810
for people to use it 24/7.

00:07:30.490 --> 00:07:33.910
Now in the early days of
designing this program, we

00:07:33.910 --> 00:07:36.670
often came back to the
idea of, can we just

00:07:36.670 --> 00:07:38.800
put Chromebooks out there
on a charging shelf,

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:40.550
and not add all these
bells and whistles--

00:07:40.550 --> 00:07:44.890
no signage, no automation, and
maybe not even Chromebooks?

00:07:44.890 --> 00:07:46.849
So that's when we started
to dig into, well,

00:07:46.849 --> 00:07:49.390
beyond those four core tenets,
what things are really, really

00:07:49.390 --> 00:07:53.710
important to us in
building out this program?

00:07:53.710 --> 00:07:55.172
So the first of those things--

00:07:55.172 --> 00:07:57.130
the first key ingredient
that we had identified

00:07:57.130 --> 00:08:00.724
was, Chromebooks really adds
so much to the equation.

00:08:00.724 --> 00:08:03.140
Here's a list of a few things
that we think are important.

00:08:03.140 --> 00:08:06.695
The first is, Chromebooks
scale really, really easily.

00:08:06.695 --> 00:08:08.297
They're super easy to set up.

00:08:08.297 --> 00:08:09.880
It takes about 10
minutes, on average,

00:08:09.880 --> 00:08:11.320
to deploy a Chromebook.

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:13.154
And we can add
devices to the fleet

00:08:13.154 --> 00:08:15.070
very quickly because of
that and kind of scale

00:08:15.070 --> 00:08:18.030
up how many devices
were out there.

00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:20.271
In addition, security
was top-of-mind for us.

00:08:20.271 --> 00:08:22.770
These devices weren't going to
see IT admins all that often.

00:08:22.770 --> 00:08:24.840
So we wanted to make sure
they were super secure.

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:27.444
Things like encrypted profiles,
automatic wipe, verified boot,

00:08:27.444 --> 00:08:29.610
and sign-in restrictions
are really important to us.

00:08:29.610 --> 00:08:31.568
We'll dig into those
actually a little bit more

00:08:31.568 --> 00:08:32.970
on the next slide.

00:08:32.970 --> 00:08:35.409
Switching easily between
devices was also very important.

00:08:35.409 --> 00:08:38.502
So the best experience would
be, if you use a Chrome

00:08:38.502 --> 00:08:40.210
device as your primary
device, and you're

00:08:40.210 --> 00:08:43.490
using a Chrome device as
a loner, it was seamless.

00:08:43.490 --> 00:08:45.559
At Google, we use the
Chrome browser quite a bit.

00:08:45.559 --> 00:08:47.850
So that made it easy for our
people on other platforms.

00:08:47.850 --> 00:08:50.580
So if you are using the Chrome
browser on, say, a Windows

00:08:50.580 --> 00:08:51.310
machine--

00:08:51.310 --> 00:08:55.350
logging into a loaner Chromebook
was a very familiar experience.

00:08:55.350 --> 00:08:57.090
On top of that,
Chrome Sync made it

00:08:57.090 --> 00:08:59.910
so that things like bookmarks,
and data, and settings also

00:08:59.910 --> 00:09:03.710
made it onto that device,
making it a very familiar feel.

00:09:03.710 --> 00:09:05.190
Grab and Go Chromebook models--

00:09:05.190 --> 00:09:08.630
we found that picking
very durable models

00:09:08.630 --> 00:09:10.780
was important to the
program being successful.

00:09:10.780 --> 00:09:13.560
So we wanted
enterprise-grade devices

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:15.930
so that our users could be
as productive as possible.

00:09:15.930 --> 00:09:18.760
But we found what we called
the rental car effect.

00:09:18.760 --> 00:09:20.410
So if you are using
a loaner device,

00:09:20.410 --> 00:09:22.410
you didn't really treat
it the same as you would

00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:23.085
your own device.

00:09:23.085 --> 00:09:24.960
And we wanted to make
sure all of our devices

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:26.960
could stand up to that
wear-and-tear of a loaner

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:27.630
program.

00:09:27.630 --> 00:09:29.745
And last but certainly
not least, Chrome OS

00:09:29.745 --> 00:09:31.770
is loaner program
ready out of the box.

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:33.674
The features that I
talked about earlier do

00:09:33.674 --> 00:09:34.590
need to be configured.

00:09:34.590 --> 00:09:36.881
But it just takes a few clicks
in the Chrome management

00:09:36.881 --> 00:09:38.440
applications.

00:09:38.440 --> 00:09:40.860
So let's talk about
those security

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:43.620
features that are
so important to us.

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:46.380
First, there is system updates
being automatically downloaded

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:48.400
and installed on every device.

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:50.480
This was a great
piece-of-mind feature for us.

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:52.590
And it made it so
that we didn't need

00:09:52.590 --> 00:09:54.960
to worry about taking these
devices in, updating them.

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:58.650
It would all happen while they
were out working with users.

00:09:58.650 --> 00:10:00.240
Only users on a
specified domain can

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:01.680
log in to our loaner devices.

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:04.020
So only Google.com
users can log in.

00:10:04.020 --> 00:10:05.864
And we have guest mode
disabled by default,

00:10:05.864 --> 00:10:06.780
but you can enable it.

00:10:06.780 --> 00:10:09.090
We'll talk about
that more later.

00:10:09.090 --> 00:10:11.280
Policy enforcement-- even
after factory reset--

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:12.120
was also important.

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:14.759
We didn't want devices to walk
off, someone to factory reset

00:10:14.759 --> 00:10:16.050
it, and then it's their device.

00:10:16.050 --> 00:10:17.841
We wanted it to stay
in the loaner program.

00:10:17.841 --> 00:10:20.040
So auto domain re-enrollment
was a key feature

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:21.780
that we relied on.

00:10:21.780 --> 00:10:25.770
User data being encrypted
between the different profiles

00:10:25.770 --> 00:10:26.451
was also key.

00:10:26.451 --> 00:10:28.450
We needed these devices
to go from user to user,

00:10:28.450 --> 00:10:29.730
as I mentioned before.

00:10:29.730 --> 00:10:33.287
And lastly, Chrome OS has
baked in tamper protection.

00:10:33.287 --> 00:10:34.370
It's called verified boot.

00:10:34.370 --> 00:10:35.940
And that makes sure that
there's nothing funny

00:10:35.940 --> 00:10:37.920
going on with the OS
already on the device.

00:10:41.130 --> 00:10:44.050
Design considerations-- so
there are three key ones here.

00:10:44.050 --> 00:10:47.560
The first is the signage
that I mentioned before.

00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:49.770
All the signage and
stickers that we use

00:10:49.770 --> 00:10:52.920
are very intuitive, very
easy to understand just

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:54.264
in a minute of reading it.

00:10:54.264 --> 00:10:56.180
The signs, which I'll
show a little bit later,

00:10:56.180 --> 00:10:57.146
have just three steps.

00:10:57.146 --> 00:10:58.770
All the stickers just
have a few steps,

00:10:58.770 --> 00:11:00.478
so that you can quickly
understand what's

00:11:00.478 --> 00:11:02.262
going on with the program.

00:11:02.262 --> 00:11:03.970
The Chrome companion
app that I mentioned

00:11:03.970 --> 00:11:05.950
before also pops up
as soon as you log in,

00:11:05.950 --> 00:11:08.200
and gives you kind of some
handholding guidance on how

00:11:08.200 --> 00:11:10.567
to use the program, how it
works, and it eases some

00:11:10.567 --> 00:11:12.400
of those fears that
users might have-- like,

00:11:12.400 --> 00:11:15.050
do I need to check this out, do
I need to talk to an IT admin?

00:11:15.050 --> 00:11:16.760
No, it's all taken care of.

00:11:16.760 --> 00:11:19.280
And lastly, follow-up details
are emailed to the user.

00:11:19.280 --> 00:11:21.809
So in a case that users
have even more questions--

00:11:21.809 --> 00:11:24.350
maybe we want to tell them what
their return date is for that

00:11:24.350 --> 00:11:26.260
device, or maybe we want
to nag them and say, hey,

00:11:26.260 --> 00:11:28.030
you've held onto this
for a little too long--

00:11:28.030 --> 00:11:29.571
all of that is
facilitated via email.

00:11:32.740 --> 00:11:36.287
Automation-- this is another
key piece of the experience.

00:11:36.287 --> 00:11:38.620
Now I've mentioned the Chrome
companion app a few times.

00:11:38.620 --> 00:11:40.450
But the whole
brain of the system

00:11:40.450 --> 00:11:42.970
is a custom App Engine
application that we built,

00:11:42.970 --> 00:11:44.660
that drives the whole program.

00:11:44.660 --> 00:11:47.680
Now this makes management of
the program super easy and just

00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:49.570
about hands-off.

00:11:49.570 --> 00:11:52.480
That backend application
tracks device usage,

00:11:52.480 --> 00:11:55.540
it tracks availability
of each charging rack,

00:11:55.540 --> 00:11:57.640
and it also notifies
the users if they

00:11:57.640 --> 00:11:59.050
need to return the device.

00:11:59.050 --> 00:12:02.710
Maybe they need to extend
it, or extend their loan,

00:12:02.710 --> 00:12:05.220
or keep it for a
little bit longer.

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:06.950
Let's dig into this
a little bit more.

00:12:06.950 --> 00:12:09.744
So the first piece of this
automation that's vitally

00:12:09.744 --> 00:12:10.910
important is the Chromebook.

00:12:10.910 --> 00:12:12.050
We've already covered that.

00:12:12.050 --> 00:12:13.700
The baked-in features
make Chrome OS

00:12:13.700 --> 00:12:17.120
an easy and very important
part of the program.

00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:19.730
Google's admin APIs allow
us to enable key features

00:12:19.730 --> 00:12:22.200
that keep those
devices safe, secure,

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:23.950
and usable by our users.

00:12:23.950 --> 00:12:25.700
And then one of the
policies that's pushed

00:12:25.700 --> 00:12:27.350
is actually that
Chrome companion app.

00:12:27.350 --> 00:12:29.900
You can push
applications via policy.

00:12:29.900 --> 00:12:33.230
When that application installs--
as soon as the user logs in--

00:12:33.230 --> 00:12:36.590
it then phones home to
the App Engine backend,

00:12:36.590 --> 00:12:38.974
where reporting is done,
where emails are sent.

00:12:38.974 --> 00:12:40.890
We already talked about
a few of those things.

00:12:40.890 --> 00:12:43.250
The other important thing
that backend application does

00:12:43.250 --> 00:12:45.320
is integrate with things
like reporting systems

00:12:45.320 --> 00:12:48.470
to work seamlessly with
Google's BigQuery and Datastore.

00:12:48.470 --> 00:12:50.870
And it allows us to time to
things like ticketing systems

00:12:50.870 --> 00:12:52.054
that we use internally.

00:12:55.020 --> 00:12:56.430
So does this work?

00:12:56.430 --> 00:12:57.172
Yes, definitely.

00:12:57.172 --> 00:12:59.130
We've been really happy
with our loaner program

00:12:59.130 --> 00:13:00.840
internally at Google.

00:13:00.840 --> 00:13:02.472
Around a third of
all Google employees

00:13:02.472 --> 00:13:03.930
use a Grab and Go
device each year.

00:13:03.930 --> 00:13:08.430
That's roughly 275 loans
per day and growing.

00:13:08.430 --> 00:13:12.000
Around half of program uses are
to remedy a true productivity

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:13.590
blocking problem.

00:13:13.590 --> 00:13:16.560
Previously, those problems may
have taken up to a day or more

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:17.620
to resolve.

00:13:17.620 --> 00:13:19.800
So gone are the days
of waiting weeks

00:13:19.800 --> 00:13:21.970
for a replacement
device, or maybe a day

00:13:21.970 --> 00:13:24.410
while the IT department
troubleshoots your problem.

00:13:24.410 --> 00:13:27.030
Your back up and
running instantly now.

00:13:27.030 --> 00:13:30.450
The other half of usage
is more for users that

00:13:30.450 --> 00:13:32.240
want some kind of convenience.

00:13:32.240 --> 00:13:34.290
So maybe they're
running between meetings

00:13:34.290 --> 00:13:35.640
and don't want to carry
a laptop with them.

00:13:35.640 --> 00:13:36.630
Or they're traveling
and they don't

00:13:36.630 --> 00:13:38.252
want to bring their
laptop with them.

00:13:38.252 --> 00:13:39.960
We found that this is
a nice convenience.

00:13:39.960 --> 00:13:41.543
It definitely keeps
people productive.

00:13:41.543 --> 00:13:44.950
And it's also been a nice
perk for a lot of Googlers.

00:13:44.950 --> 00:13:48.020
And lastly, we estimated that
in its first year of operation,

00:13:48.020 --> 00:13:49.610
this program paid for itself--

00:13:49.610 --> 00:13:52.175
in productivity time
saved-- after only 50 days.

00:13:52.175 --> 00:13:54.050
And now that includes
our capital investment,

00:13:54.050 --> 00:13:55.925
the time we invested
into it, and everything.

00:13:55.925 --> 00:13:57.590
50 days is a great
ROI on this program.

00:14:00.980 --> 00:14:04.590
So a few of the use cases
that I mentioned earlier--

00:14:04.590 --> 00:14:06.590
so there are actually
three buckets of use cases

00:14:06.590 --> 00:14:07.298
we've identified.

00:14:07.298 --> 00:14:09.730
The first is that
productivity-blocking use case.

00:14:09.730 --> 00:14:11.630
So those are the broken
devices, the lost,

00:14:11.630 --> 00:14:14.510
the forgotten devices, the
places where you just wouldn't

00:14:14.510 --> 00:14:15.801
have been working otherwise.

00:14:15.801 --> 00:14:17.300
On the convenience
side, I mentioned

00:14:17.300 --> 00:14:20.120
there is travel, on-the-go
between meetings.

00:14:20.120 --> 00:14:21.892
But this third
category we've found

00:14:21.892 --> 00:14:24.100
is very interesting, which
is introducing Chromebooks

00:14:24.100 --> 00:14:25.539
to your organization.

00:14:25.539 --> 00:14:27.830
The Grab and Go program can
be a really interesting way

00:14:27.830 --> 00:14:31.010
to break the ice with users that
don't have a lot of experience

00:14:31.010 --> 00:14:32.090
with Chrome OS.

00:14:32.090 --> 00:14:34.430
It's a fantastic way to
show the power of Chrome OS

00:14:34.430 --> 00:14:37.200
and also kind of save
the day at the same time.

00:14:37.200 --> 00:14:39.200
So if you're looking to
expand your Chrome OS

00:14:39.200 --> 00:14:41.570
footprint at your company,
this might be a fantastic way

00:14:41.570 --> 00:14:46.850
to do it, and have an easy
win for the IT department.

00:14:46.850 --> 00:14:50.690
So why should you adopt
Chrome Enterprise Grab and Go?

00:14:50.690 --> 00:14:52.910
First and foremost,
reducing downtime.

00:14:52.910 --> 00:14:54.500
Keeping your
employees productive

00:14:54.500 --> 00:14:56.000
is top-of-mind for us.

00:14:56.000 --> 00:14:57.980
And it should be for you too.

00:14:57.980 --> 00:14:59.910
Grab and Go is also
a technology enabler.

00:14:59.910 --> 00:15:01.610
It's an easy win for
an IT department.

00:15:01.610 --> 00:15:03.620
And it makes your
users a lot happier.

00:15:03.620 --> 00:15:06.320
It removes frustration,
stress, and it's really

00:15:06.320 --> 00:15:08.060
a fantastic safety
net at the bottom

00:15:08.060 --> 00:15:11.790
of many, many broken journeys.

00:15:11.790 --> 00:15:13.020
It's super easy to implement.

00:15:13.020 --> 00:15:15.180
Chrome OS has these
fantastic features

00:15:15.180 --> 00:15:17.850
that you can enable just
by clicking a few buttons.

00:15:17.850 --> 00:15:20.070
And we've made it even
easier, which I'll give you

00:15:20.070 --> 00:15:22.440
some clues on that in a minute.

00:15:22.440 --> 00:15:24.510
And lastly, it allows
your IT department

00:15:24.510 --> 00:15:26.850
to focus on fixing problems,
rather than handing out

00:15:26.850 --> 00:15:27.720
loaners.

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:31.170
Like I said earlier, around
10% of visits to our help desk

00:15:31.170 --> 00:15:32.409
were just for loaner devices.

00:15:32.409 --> 00:15:34.200
And I'm sure that's
true at other companies

00:15:34.200 --> 00:15:36.215
offering a similar
loaner program.

00:15:36.215 --> 00:15:37.590
So this allows
your team to focus

00:15:37.590 --> 00:15:39.480
on the really
tough aspects of IT

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:45.130
and maybe the not-so-enjoyable
parts of inventory management.

00:15:45.130 --> 00:15:46.110
How to get started--

00:15:46.110 --> 00:15:47.890
we made it super easy.

00:15:47.890 --> 00:15:51.090
The first thing you should
do is go to g.co/grabandgo.

00:15:51.090 --> 00:15:53.550
We have a white paper with
lots more details on how

00:15:53.550 --> 00:15:54.854
our loaner program works.

00:15:54.854 --> 00:15:56.020
So you can give that a look.

00:15:56.020 --> 00:15:57.520
And you can also
sign up for updates

00:15:57.520 --> 00:15:58.950
on how the program
is progressing

00:15:58.950 --> 00:16:01.760
and what we're sharing
at any given time.

00:16:01.760 --> 00:16:04.630
And you can also access all of
our open source resources that

00:16:04.630 --> 00:16:08.650
we've made available on GitHub
at GitHub.com/Google/loaner.

00:16:08.650 --> 00:16:10.660
And now on GitHub,
we have everything

00:16:10.660 --> 00:16:13.847
from code, to instructions,
to setup materials,

00:16:13.847 --> 00:16:16.180
to even the images and assets
that I'm going to show you

00:16:16.180 --> 00:16:17.800
in just a minute here.

00:16:17.800 --> 00:16:19.450
So give those two things a look.

00:16:19.450 --> 00:16:23.430
And let's jump into a
couple of demos here.

00:16:23.430 --> 00:16:25.220
So the first thing
I'm going to show you

00:16:25.220 --> 00:16:29.240
is a video demo of
the companion app

00:16:29.240 --> 00:16:32.430
that I've mentioned a
couple of times here.

00:16:32.430 --> 00:16:36.990
This application that you're
about to see is the bare bones,

00:16:36.990 --> 00:16:39.960
basic, out-of-the-box
experience.

00:16:39.960 --> 00:16:43.020
This application
is incredibly easy

00:16:43.020 --> 00:16:44.835
to set up, incredibly
easy to customize.

00:16:44.835 --> 00:16:46.710
And I'll go through
where those customization

00:16:46.710 --> 00:16:47.459
options are later.

00:16:50.090 --> 00:16:52.550
First thing that happens
when you sign in,

00:16:52.550 --> 00:16:55.170
you see this
application pops up.

00:16:55.170 --> 00:16:57.720
In the bottom left-hand
corner, the icon shows up.

00:16:57.720 --> 00:17:00.350
And this reader is the
first thing that you see.

00:17:00.350 --> 00:17:01.130
Let's get started.

00:17:01.130 --> 00:17:03.150
It explains how
the program works.

00:17:03.150 --> 00:17:06.140
It gives you information on what
the program is and kind of sets

00:17:06.140 --> 00:17:07.844
you off in the right direction.

00:17:07.844 --> 00:17:09.260
The next thing
that we've included

00:17:09.260 --> 00:17:11.510
is a survey that
asks you maybe, what

00:17:11.510 --> 00:17:12.798
you're using the device for.

00:17:12.798 --> 00:17:14.339
That's one of the
things that we used

00:17:14.339 --> 00:17:16.762
to do capacity-planning at
Google for our loaner program.

00:17:16.762 --> 00:17:17.970
So we think that's important.

00:17:17.970 --> 00:17:21.050
But you can change this to
however you want it to be.

00:17:21.050 --> 00:17:23.720
Every screen that you see
here is fully customizable.

00:17:23.720 --> 00:17:25.869
The next thing is
setting a return date.

00:17:25.869 --> 00:17:27.194
Again, it's fully customizable.

00:17:27.194 --> 00:17:28.610
You can say, this
is due tomorrow.

00:17:28.610 --> 00:17:31.640
Or you can say, let us know when
you're going to bring it back.

00:17:31.640 --> 00:17:34.160
And the last thing
here before you go,

00:17:34.160 --> 00:17:35.600
this is some return
instructions.

00:17:35.600 --> 00:17:37.550
A lot of our users were
having trouble understanding

00:17:37.550 --> 00:17:38.600
how to return the device.

00:17:38.600 --> 00:17:39.840
It was a little bit too easy.

00:17:39.840 --> 00:17:41.798
So we wanted to give some
instructions-- again,

00:17:41.798 --> 00:17:43.880
fully-customizable here.

00:17:43.880 --> 00:17:46.060
So this is the persistent
app experience.

00:17:46.060 --> 00:17:48.070
You can get here by
launching the application

00:17:48.070 --> 00:17:49.070
by clicking on the icon.

00:17:49.070 --> 00:17:50.486
And you have a
couple of functions

00:17:50.486 --> 00:17:51.590
that you can perform here.

00:17:51.590 --> 00:17:53.330
One is extending the loan.

00:17:53.330 --> 00:17:56.800
So you can click into it,
pick your new return date,

00:17:56.800 --> 00:17:59.790
and tell us when you're
bringing it back.

00:17:59.790 --> 00:18:02.226
The next thing that you
can do is enable guest mode

00:18:02.226 --> 00:18:02.850
on this device.

00:18:02.850 --> 00:18:05.820
And as I mentioned, by
default, we disable guest mode.

00:18:05.820 --> 00:18:07.480
But we found that
a lot of Googlers

00:18:07.480 --> 00:18:09.480
wanted to use these devices
to facilitate things

00:18:09.480 --> 00:18:10.980
like interviews, and
have a candidate do

00:18:10.980 --> 00:18:12.300
some coding on the Chromebook.

00:18:12.300 --> 00:18:15.760
So enabling guest mode was a
nice way to accommodate that.

00:18:15.760 --> 00:18:18.180
And then lastly, you can
mark the device as returned.

00:18:18.180 --> 00:18:20.520
This helps program
administrators know

00:18:20.520 --> 00:18:22.100
this device is on its way back.

00:18:22.100 --> 00:18:25.500
It also helps that user to
close the loop on the loan.

00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:28.490
It gives them that last
prompt to bring it back--

00:18:28.490 --> 00:18:30.356
again, return
instructions, helping them

00:18:30.356 --> 00:18:31.980
understand how to
bring this back, what

00:18:31.980 --> 00:18:34.280
they should be doing.

00:18:34.280 --> 00:18:37.020
In the next view here
that you're going to see,

00:18:37.020 --> 00:18:38.160
there's another survey.

00:18:38.160 --> 00:18:40.722
This is a general CSAT survey.

00:18:40.722 --> 00:18:42.180
You can put whatever
you want here.

00:18:42.180 --> 00:18:44.530
We found that CSAT was useful.

00:18:44.530 --> 00:18:45.505
And are you sure?

00:18:45.505 --> 00:18:47.880
Just confirming, are you going
to bring the device back--

00:18:47.880 --> 00:18:48.963
put it back on the shelf--

00:18:48.963 --> 00:18:50.960
again, fully customizable.

00:18:50.960 --> 00:18:52.390
And that's it.

00:18:52.390 --> 00:18:56.950
So the next thing the user is
going to do is close the lid,

00:18:56.950 --> 00:19:02.170
bring it back to the shelf,
plug it in, and they're all set.

00:19:02.170 --> 00:19:04.930
The next thing I'm going to
show is a welcome animation.

00:19:04.930 --> 00:19:08.590
Now this animation is
something that we've

00:19:08.590 --> 00:19:10.810
decided that we want to
show in that application.

00:19:10.810 --> 00:19:12.970
Instead of that
static text, we found

00:19:12.970 --> 00:19:15.460
that animations for key
components of the workflow

00:19:15.460 --> 00:19:17.231
can be a little
bit more inviting

00:19:17.231 --> 00:19:19.480
and will communicate key
information a little bit more

00:19:19.480 --> 00:19:21.117
effectively.

00:19:21.117 --> 00:19:22.700
Both of these
animations and the shelf

00:19:22.700 --> 00:19:24.199
sign that I am going
to show you are

00:19:24.199 --> 00:19:30.930
provided open source on the Grab
and Go application by default.

00:19:30.930 --> 00:19:33.810
Welcome, inviting image,
let's get started.

00:19:33.810 --> 00:19:36.990
It gives you a quick walkthrough
of the app experience itself,

00:19:36.990 --> 00:19:39.990
enabling guest mode,
extending loan duration,

00:19:39.990 --> 00:19:43.180
and accessing the FAQ if
you have more questions.

00:19:43.180 --> 00:19:45.100
You can go into to
the app at any time.

00:19:47.534 --> 00:19:49.200
And when you're ready
to return-- again,

00:19:49.200 --> 00:19:50.866
we want to reinforce--
click that button

00:19:50.866 --> 00:19:52.800
and bring it back to us.

00:19:52.800 --> 00:19:56.360
And this is just looping
again that you see here.

00:19:56.360 --> 00:19:58.710
The next animation
that I'm going to show

00:19:58.710 --> 00:20:00.180
is for the return flow.

00:20:04.140 --> 00:20:07.740
Before you return it, there's
a couple things we need to do.

00:20:07.740 --> 00:20:09.630
The first, we found
that a lot of our users

00:20:09.630 --> 00:20:12.160
were leaving USB devices
plugged into their device.

00:20:12.160 --> 00:20:15.190
So we tell them
to pull those out.

00:20:15.190 --> 00:20:17.694
Bring it back to the
shelf, plug-in the device.

00:20:17.694 --> 00:20:19.860
Having it fully charged for
the next user is useful.

00:20:19.860 --> 00:20:23.150
If you do distribute chargers
with your loaners, return that.

00:20:23.150 --> 00:20:25.670
And you're all set.

00:20:25.670 --> 00:20:28.750
Now again, these animations
can be shown in the app itself

00:20:28.750 --> 00:20:30.020
as a part of the experience.

00:20:30.020 --> 00:20:32.144
And we think that's a great
way to really reinforce

00:20:32.144 --> 00:20:33.810
these key points.

00:20:33.810 --> 00:20:36.620
And the last piece here
is actually the shelf sign

00:20:36.620 --> 00:20:38.199
that we recommend using.

00:20:38.199 --> 00:20:39.740
You can see this
just linked directly

00:20:39.740 --> 00:20:41.360
to our GitHub repository.

00:20:41.360 --> 00:20:44.280
And this is a really, really
easy flow to understand.

00:20:44.280 --> 00:20:47.000
You grab a device, you log
in, and you get back to work.

00:20:47.000 --> 00:20:48.650
It's as simple as that.

00:20:48.650 --> 00:20:51.830
All the stickers that we
recommend using for our devices

00:20:51.830 --> 00:20:53.930
and understanding the
program a little bit better

00:20:53.930 --> 00:20:58.320
are also in the same
repository on our GitHub repo.

00:20:58.320 --> 00:21:02.970
So that's about all I've got
in the demo space for you.

00:21:02.970 --> 00:21:04.450
That's a wrap.

00:21:04.450 --> 00:21:05.300
Stay tuned.

00:21:05.300 --> 00:21:08.546
We're going to switch over to a
live QA in just a few minutes.

00:21:08.546 --> 00:21:09.670
Thanks for watching so far.

00:21:09.670 --> 00:21:10.503
We'll be right back.

00:22:58.430 --> 00:23:00.380
Thanks for sticking with us.

00:23:00.380 --> 00:23:02.711
I think we're going to
switch over to questions now.

00:23:02.711 --> 00:23:04.460
So the first one that
I see that's come in

00:23:04.460 --> 00:23:06.970
is, how many devices
should I plan

00:23:06.970 --> 00:23:10.820
to buy for my company's
Grab and Go program?

00:23:10.820 --> 00:23:13.250
We don't have a specific
number that we recommend.

00:23:13.250 --> 00:23:15.380
Generally, there are ratios
that you can stick to.

00:23:15.380 --> 00:23:18.470
But every business, every
enterprise, every use case

00:23:18.470 --> 00:23:20.296
is going to be a
little bit different.

00:23:20.296 --> 00:23:21.920
We found that some
companies don't even

00:23:21.920 --> 00:23:24.060
want to use this program
as a loaner program.

00:23:24.060 --> 00:23:26.300
It's more just a
shared fleet of devices

00:23:26.300 --> 00:23:28.592
for maybe manufacturing
line workers,

00:23:28.592 --> 00:23:29.550
or something like that.

00:23:29.550 --> 00:23:31.470
So keep the use case in mind.

00:23:31.470 --> 00:23:33.800
But for a loaner program,
what we recommend

00:23:33.800 --> 00:23:37.130
is somewhere between one
device for every 30 employees

00:23:37.130 --> 00:23:39.660
and one device for
every 50 employees.

00:23:39.660 --> 00:23:43.179
So the 1 to 50 ratio will
keep your fleet pretty slim.

00:23:43.179 --> 00:23:44.720
And it's going to
be highly utilized.

00:23:44.720 --> 00:23:46.667
And you might have some outages.

00:23:46.667 --> 00:23:48.500
1 to 30, you'll add a
little bit more slack.

00:23:48.500 --> 00:23:50.750
And you might see more of
that kind of convenience

00:23:50.750 --> 00:23:52.550
usage creeping in.

00:23:52.550 --> 00:23:53.690
For us, that's OK.

00:23:53.690 --> 00:23:56.575
We actually tend to stray
more toward the 1 to 30

00:23:56.575 --> 00:23:57.200
side of things.

00:23:57.200 --> 00:23:59.460
But it's constantly
changing at Google.

00:23:59.460 --> 00:24:01.850
So in summary, do what's
best for your company.

00:24:01.850 --> 00:24:03.897
And I would say start small.

00:24:03.897 --> 00:24:05.480
Put a couple of
deployments out there.

00:24:05.480 --> 00:24:06.420
See how they are performing.

00:24:06.420 --> 00:24:07.400
Adjust the numbers.

00:24:07.400 --> 00:24:11.560
Find your min and max
numbers and go from there.

00:24:11.560 --> 00:24:15.461
Next up, can I deploy a VDI
solution with Grab and Go?

00:24:15.461 --> 00:24:15.960
Yes.

00:24:15.960 --> 00:24:18.520
So that's definitely possible.

00:24:18.520 --> 00:24:20.550
We think that the
beauty of this program

00:24:20.550 --> 00:24:22.830
is that it's kind
of just Chrome OS.

00:24:22.830 --> 00:24:25.590
And then we've added some great
management features and user

00:24:25.590 --> 00:24:27.630
experiences on top of it.

00:24:27.630 --> 00:24:31.530
So it should be adaptable
unless you've got an outlier

00:24:31.530 --> 00:24:33.000
setup with your VDI solution.

00:24:33.000 --> 00:24:34.470
But all of this should work.

00:24:34.470 --> 00:24:37.260
And we've actually heard
of some customers planning

00:24:37.260 --> 00:24:39.887
to use this as almost a
thin client replacement

00:24:39.887 --> 00:24:40.720
for their employees.

00:24:40.720 --> 00:24:44.070
So definitely give it a go.

00:24:44.070 --> 00:24:48.270
Can I recreate this
for my small business?

00:24:48.270 --> 00:24:50.070
Yes, definitely.

00:24:50.070 --> 00:24:52.020
One of the beautiful
things about open

00:24:52.020 --> 00:24:55.260
sourcing this is that anyone
can pick it up and run with it.

00:24:55.260 --> 00:24:57.314
There is a minimal
amount of effort

00:24:57.314 --> 00:24:59.730
that you'd have to put in to
get the out-of-box experience

00:24:59.730 --> 00:25:00.229
working.

00:25:00.229 --> 00:25:03.366
But this scales from even a
small business all the way up

00:25:03.366 --> 00:25:04.990
to a large enterprise
just like Google.

00:25:04.990 --> 00:25:07.390
So give it a go.

00:25:07.390 --> 00:25:10.070
Do you supply chargers
with Chromebook loans?

00:25:10.070 --> 00:25:11.840
So initially we did.

00:25:11.840 --> 00:25:14.850
Now we actually don't
for a couple of reasons.

00:25:14.850 --> 00:25:19.237
The first and biggest reason is
that those chargers disappeared

00:25:19.237 --> 00:25:21.570
really, really quickly in the
early days of the program.

00:25:21.570 --> 00:25:25.340
So we were giving out
chargers and devices

00:25:25.340 --> 00:25:27.210
kind of on a one-for-one basis.

00:25:27.210 --> 00:25:29.630
We got all the devices
back, but we got about 10%

00:25:29.630 --> 00:25:31.110
of the chargers back.

00:25:31.110 --> 00:25:34.140
And chargers are not cheap,
especially on those numbers.

00:25:34.140 --> 00:25:36.170
So initially, we
elected to kind of scale

00:25:36.170 --> 00:25:38.760
back how many charges
we were giving out.

00:25:38.760 --> 00:25:43.880
Later, the company
aligned on USB-C charging.

00:25:43.880 --> 00:25:46.160
And we kind of took our cue
from the broader company

00:25:46.160 --> 00:25:49.220
and said, let's align all
of our devices in the loaner

00:25:49.220 --> 00:25:51.380
program on USB-C
charging so that people

00:25:51.380 --> 00:25:52.340
don't need chargers.

00:25:52.340 --> 00:25:56.120
Typically, Googlers will have
a USB-C charger at their desk,

00:25:56.120 --> 00:25:58.190
in conference rooms,
wherever they're working.

00:25:58.190 --> 00:26:02.120
So they no longer needed
a charger in those cases.

00:26:02.120 --> 00:26:04.380
That was a nice, happy
accident in the end.

00:26:04.380 --> 00:26:07.640
But I would say if you don't
have that same charging

00:26:07.640 --> 00:26:11.030
environment for your entire
company, start conservative.

00:26:11.030 --> 00:26:15.440
And make sure that you're not
spending too much on chargers

00:26:15.440 --> 00:26:19.080
like we did in the early days.

00:26:19.080 --> 00:26:21.150
Do I need to use
GCP for Grab and Go?

00:26:21.150 --> 00:26:22.740
Out of the box, yes.

00:26:22.740 --> 00:26:25.800
We use GCP, we recommend
GCP, the integrations

00:26:25.800 --> 00:26:27.046
are super easy.

00:26:27.046 --> 00:26:28.920
But again, the beauty
of it being open source

00:26:28.920 --> 00:26:31.320
is that you can adapt this
to just about any use case.

00:26:31.320 --> 00:26:34.530
If you don't want to use GCP,
pull out the GCP components

00:26:34.530 --> 00:26:38.070
and add in the
ones that you want.

00:26:38.070 --> 00:26:40.890
What do you do about users that
don't return their Chromebook?

00:26:40.890 --> 00:26:44.940
Can you talk more about how
you communicate with users?

00:26:44.940 --> 00:26:49.020
So somewhere in the neighborhood
of 2% or 3% of loans

00:26:49.020 --> 00:26:53.156
go into kind of like a lost
or a super long loan state.

00:26:53.156 --> 00:26:55.530
For those users, we have a
couple of different approaches

00:26:55.530 --> 00:26:57.930
that we're experimenting
with right now.

00:26:57.930 --> 00:27:00.240
The first and one that's
baked-in by default

00:27:00.240 --> 00:27:02.820
with the Grab and Go
program is nag mail.

00:27:02.820 --> 00:27:05.700
So we'll send reminder emails
to bring the device back

00:27:05.700 --> 00:27:06.970
on a recurring basis.

00:27:06.970 --> 00:27:10.390
And we found that those
are surprisingly effective.

00:27:10.390 --> 00:27:12.240
We've also experimented
with locking

00:27:12.240 --> 00:27:16.440
devices that have gone missing,
but are still being used.

00:27:16.440 --> 00:27:19.650
Or also copying
managers on nag mails.

00:27:19.650 --> 00:27:22.670
Both appear to be
just as effective.

00:27:22.670 --> 00:27:24.934
If we're not seeing any
activity on the device--

00:27:24.934 --> 00:27:26.850
if nobody's logging in,
if nobody's using it--

00:27:26.850 --> 00:27:27.930
we consider it lost.

00:27:27.930 --> 00:27:31.410
And the Chrome management suite
has these really great features

00:27:31.410 --> 00:27:33.720
that allow you to essentially
lock-out a device.

00:27:33.720 --> 00:27:36.150
We do that for all
of those devices.

00:27:36.150 --> 00:27:37.680
If we're still
seeing activity even

00:27:37.680 --> 00:27:41.100
after those, maybe, manager
escalations and nag mails,

00:27:41.100 --> 00:27:42.160
we'll reach out directly.

00:27:42.160 --> 00:27:45.210
And we're starting programs
now where we're reaching out

00:27:45.210 --> 00:27:46.770
and we're finding
that a lot of teams

00:27:46.770 --> 00:27:49.260
that are hanging onto Grab
and Go devices for a long time

00:27:49.260 --> 00:27:52.419
have a really fantastic use
case for just using Chrome OS.

00:27:52.419 --> 00:27:54.210
So what we're doing in
a lot of those cases

00:27:54.210 --> 00:27:56.400
is just buying Chromebooks
for those teams

00:27:56.400 --> 00:27:59.010
and solving the problems that
they're having and also getting

00:27:59.010 --> 00:28:00.135
our loaners back from them.

00:28:02.560 --> 00:28:05.620
How technical do I need to
be to deploy Grab and Go?

00:28:05.620 --> 00:28:06.350
Quite technical.

00:28:06.350 --> 00:28:09.244
But there's a bit of a
spectrum that exists here.

00:28:09.244 --> 00:28:11.785
So if you want all the bells
and whistles that come with Grab

00:28:11.785 --> 00:28:13.630
and Go-- yes, it is a
technical deployment

00:28:13.630 --> 00:28:16.049
and it's going to require
you to know your stuff.

00:28:16.049 --> 00:28:18.340
But if you want to start
small-- really, really small--

00:28:18.340 --> 00:28:20.050
you don't need all those
bells and whistles.

00:28:20.050 --> 00:28:22.460
You can put Chromebooks maybe
at your help desk and say,

00:28:22.460 --> 00:28:25.170
hey, you know you're
working with an IT tech,

00:28:25.170 --> 00:28:27.490
and it's taking a long time
to get your problem solved,

00:28:27.490 --> 00:28:28.810
just grab a Chromebook.

00:28:28.810 --> 00:28:30.697
That's a fantastic
way to get started.

00:28:30.697 --> 00:28:32.530
At Google, we wanted
the bells and whistles.

00:28:32.530 --> 00:28:34.120
We wanted the program
to manage itself.

00:28:34.120 --> 00:28:35.720
And we wanted it to
be very hands-off,

00:28:35.720 --> 00:28:39.730
so we built this big program
that allowed us to do that.

00:28:39.730 --> 00:28:42.700
So how technical do you need
to be for the full bells

00:28:42.700 --> 00:28:43.930
and whistles?

00:28:43.930 --> 00:28:47.380
We recommend that a systems
administrator is involved.

00:28:47.380 --> 00:28:48.760
There is some GCP work.

00:28:48.760 --> 00:28:51.270
You need to know
Python, TypeScript.

00:28:51.270 --> 00:28:53.470
You need to be comfortable
working with App Engine

00:28:53.470 --> 00:28:55.630
applications,
Chrome applications,

00:28:55.630 --> 00:28:57.850
and maybe making some
minor adjustments

00:28:57.850 --> 00:28:59.582
to the open-source application.

00:28:59.582 --> 00:29:01.540
If you want to go even
further and customize it

00:29:01.540 --> 00:29:03.160
for your organization,
you might want

00:29:03.160 --> 00:29:05.914
to do that too and bring
in some developers.

00:29:05.914 --> 00:29:06.830
It's really up to you.

00:29:06.830 --> 00:29:09.810
But just know that there
is a minimum requirement

00:29:09.810 --> 00:29:13.354
of technical skill here.

00:29:13.354 --> 00:29:15.770
Am I able to work with a partner
to implement Grab and Go?

00:29:15.770 --> 00:29:18.510
Actually, a great segue here.

00:29:18.510 --> 00:29:21.620
So if you do want a
Grab and Go program,

00:29:21.620 --> 00:29:23.420
but you don't have
the technical skills--

00:29:23.420 --> 00:29:25.961
or maybe you just don't have
the time to customize and set up

00:29:25.961 --> 00:29:27.140
this application yourself--

00:29:27.140 --> 00:29:29.270
we do recommend that you
go and talk to the partner

00:29:29.270 --> 00:29:32.390
that you've worked with and
ask them about Grab and Go.

00:29:32.390 --> 00:29:35.330
We're also in the process
of training several close

00:29:35.330 --> 00:29:38.049
partners of Google's
and showing them the way

00:29:38.049 --> 00:29:40.340
that Google does Grab and Go
so that they can reproduce

00:29:40.340 --> 00:29:41.340
that for customers.

00:29:41.340 --> 00:29:43.259
So again, if you
fit that mold of,

00:29:43.259 --> 00:29:45.050
you don't want to do
all these deployments,

00:29:45.050 --> 00:29:47.466
you just want to ask a partner
to do it-- definitely reach

00:29:47.466 --> 00:29:50.240
out, have that conversation,
and get things going.

00:29:50.240 --> 00:29:53.120
We're expecting to be able
to publish a list of partners

00:29:53.120 --> 00:29:56.360
that we've worked with and that
are ready to deploy Grab and Go

00:29:56.360 --> 00:29:59.030
in the next few months.

00:29:59.030 --> 00:30:03.040
So I think those are all
the questions that I've had.

00:30:03.040 --> 00:30:05.779
Thank you very much
for joining us.

00:30:05.779 --> 00:30:07.570
You should stay tuned
for the next session.

00:30:07.570 --> 00:30:11.080
That's going to be CE TV,
First Steps with Apache Kafka

00:30:11.080 --> 00:30:13.480
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:30:13.480 --> 00:30:16.660
Thank you again,
and have a good one.

00:30:16.660 --> 00:30:19.710
[MUSIC PLAYING]

